INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - ATTORNEY-CLIENT ROOM - MORNING

A grey cube. Metal table. Two chairs. A buzzing fluorescent.

ARTEMIS CROSS, forties, public defender, sits with a thick file, pen clicking. She doesn't look up.

The door buzzes and swings open. Chains. A shuffle.

ROLLAND "ROLLY" PEARCE, twenties, county orange, cuffs front, wide shoulders, thin voice, enters. The door slams behind him.

ARTEMIS
Sit down, Rolly.

ROLLY
Ms. Cross.

ARTEMIS
Artemis is fine.

ROLLY
Ms. Cross is fine.

He sits. She slides a photograph across the table. He doesn't look at it.

ARTEMIS
Do you know that man?

ROLLY
No.

ARTEMIS
He says he knows you.

ROLLY
People say things.

ARTEMIS
He said you were at the Dockside on the twelfth.

ROLLY
I was home.

ARTEMIS
Alone?

ROLLY
With my cat.

ARTEMIS
Rolly, the cat can't testify.

ROLLY
Then you're in trouble.

She stops clicking. Locks eyes with him.

ARTEMIS
Listen to me. The DA offers eight years. Eight, Rolly. If we go to trial and lose, it's twenty.

ROLLY
And if we win?

ARTEMIS
You walk.

ROLLY
Then we win.

ARTEMIS
That's not how it works.

ROLLY
That's how I want it to work.

She flips a page. Reads. Turns another.

ARTEMIS
The man in the photo recanted this morning.

ROLLY
(straightening)
What?

ARTEMIS
The witness changed his story.

ROLLY
That's good.

ARTEMIS
It's complicated, because someone leaned on him. And I think you know who.

Rolly stares at the table. The fluorescent buzzes.

ROLLY
Ms. Cross. If I say a name, can you keep it in this room?

ARTEMIS
That's the whole point of the room.

He leans forward. She lays down the pen.
